WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.984 --> 00:00:03.968 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:04.960 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:20.358 align:middle line:90%


00:00:20.358 --> 00:00:22.400 align:middle line:84%
CASEY RODRIGUEZ: We defined
the Lebesgue integral

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:36.480 align:middle line:84%
for simple functions, which have
this canonical representation

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.450 align:middle line:84%
as a finite linear combination
of indicator functions

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.390 align:middle line:84%
on sets which are
pairwise disjoint

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:51.220 align:middle line:84%
and whose union gives me E.
We defined their integral--

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.210 align:middle line:90%


00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:00.960 align:middle line:84%
the integral of phi was defined
to be the sum from j equals 1

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:10.290 align:middle line:84%
to n aj times the measure
of capital Aj sum as j

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.340 align:middle line:90%
goes from 1 to n.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.230 align:middle line:84%
And we proved a couple
of properties of this.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:18.120 align:middle line:84%
Namely, we proved that if I
multiply a simple function

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.950 align:middle line:84%
by a non-negative
scalar, then the scalar

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.033 align:middle line:90%
pulls out of the integral.

00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:22.920 align:middle line:84%
The integral of the
scalar multiple of phi

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.490 align:middle line:84%
is equal to scalar
multiple of the integral.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.880 align:middle line:84%
If I have two non-negative
simple functions

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.580 align:middle line:84%
and I add them, I get another
non-negative simple function

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.220 align:middle line:84%
and the integral of the sum
is the sum of the integrals.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:40.560 align:middle line:84%
And we also prove that if I
have two simple functions,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.360 align:middle line:84%
one less than or
equal to the other,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.730 align:middle line:84%
then the integrals
respect this inequality.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.170 align:middle line:84%
The integral of
the smaller one is

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.810 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal to the
integral of the bigger one.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.320 align:middle line:90%


00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.410 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're going
to define the Lebesgue

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:02.520 align:middle line:84%
integral for a general
non-negative measurable

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.440 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:12.180 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, how
one should view

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.620 align:middle line:84%
the Lebesgue is kind of how
one should view the Riemann

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.150 align:middle line:90%
integral, I guess, if you--

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.370 align:middle line:84%
or at least when you think
about the Riemann integral

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:26.130 align:middle line:84%
as you build up these
approximations to the integral

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:30.180 align:middle line:84%
by cutting up the domain
for the Riemann integral.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.870 align:middle line:84%
And you choose points in between
and you form these boxes.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:37.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, you have some freedom in
how you choose these boxes that

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:41.160 align:middle line:90%
approximate the integral of f.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.660 align:middle line:84%
But one way to
choose the boxes--

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.285 align:middle line:84%
or at least when I
picture it, I always

00:02:44.285 --> 00:02:48.810 align:middle line:84%
picture the boxes sitting
below the graph of f.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.110 align:middle line:84%
And as you dice up the
domain smaller and smaller

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.530 align:middle line:90%
then these things are kind of--

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.290 align:middle line:84%
these boxes are getting narrower
and kind of your approximation

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:04.510 align:middle line:84%
is filling in the integral or
the area underneath the curve

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:05.140 align:middle line:90%
from below.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.880 align:middle line:90%


00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.260 align:middle line:84%
And we've already seen that
for non-negative measurable

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:11.800 align:middle line:90%
functions.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.730 align:middle line:84%
There always exists a
sequence of simple functions

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.200 align:middle line:90%
that increase to f.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.533 align:middle line:84%
So that for every x I stick
into the sequence of functions--

00:03:21.533 --> 00:03:23.950 align:middle line:84%
phi 1 is less than or equal
to phi 2 is less than or equal

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.250 align:middle line:90%
to phi 3 and so on--

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.330 align:middle line:84%
and these phis are
increasing pointwise to f.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:32.830 align:middle line:84%
So if you're trying to
build this on your own,

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.440 align:middle line:84%
you would think, OK, let
me define the integral as

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.140 align:middle line:84%
of a function, of a non-negative
measurable function,

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:45.340 align:middle line:84%
as the limit of the integrals of
a sequence of simple functions

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.240 align:middle line:90%
increasing to f.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.030 align:middle line:84%
Which I know exists,
because we constructed one.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.000 align:middle line:84%
Of course-- and that's
one way to do it,

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.640 align:middle line:90%
and some textbooks do that.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.820 align:middle line:84%
But you come across this problem
that this number that you've

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.640 align:middle line:84%
defined to be the limit of
these integrals-- maybe it

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.950 align:middle line:84%
depends on the sequence
of measurable functions

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.990 align:middle line:90%
that you took in the beginning.

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:11.170 align:middle line:84%
So we're not going
to quite do that.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.690 align:middle line:84%
In the end, we'll see that
this number we defined

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:19.600 align:middle line:84%
can be given as of the
limit of the integrals

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:20.870 align:middle line:90%
of simple functions.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:31.630 align:middle line:84%
So for a general non-negative
measurable functions,

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:37.680 align:middle line:84%
we define the
integral of f over E

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.290 align:middle line:84%
to be the sup of the
integral over E of phi,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:53.950 align:middle line:84%
where now, phi is a
non-negative simple function

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.515 align:middle line:90%
and phi sits below f.

00:04:56.515 --> 00:05:00.688 align:middle line:90%


00:05:00.688 --> 00:05:05.130 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, this is kind
of like taking the integral

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:10.115 align:middle line:90%
of f to be defined as kind of--

00:05:10.115 --> 00:05:12.240 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to say limit,
because it's not exactly

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.660 align:middle line:84%
a limit, but in some sense, the
thing that's getting filled up

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:19.020 align:middle line:84%
by all the integrals
of the simple functions

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.900 align:middle line:84%
below the graph that sit
below the graph of f.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:27.125 align:middle line:90%


00:05:27.125 --> 00:05:32.000 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let me just prove
a very simple theorem,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.250 align:middle line:90%
which is useful.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:41.880 align:middle line:84%
So if E is a subset
of R is measurable--

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:50.180 align:middle line:84%
in fact, it's a
set of measures 0.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.120 align:middle line:84%
So remember, all sets of outer
measure 0 are measurable.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.140 align:middle line:84%
So I don't have to say
really if E is a subset of R

00:05:57.140 --> 00:05:57.890 align:middle line:90%
with E measurable.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.182 align:middle line:84%
When I say this, I'm kind of
saying two things at once.

00:06:00.182 --> 00:06:03.620 align:middle line:84%
It's outer measure
of E is 0 or 1,

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:05.330 align:middle line:90%
and therefore it's measurable.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:09.490 align:middle line:90%


00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:15.100 align:middle line:84%
So then for all f that are
non-negative and measurable

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.510 align:middle line:84%
on E, the integral
over E of f is 0.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:21.940 align:middle line:84%
OK, so it's only kind
of interesting to take

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:27.250 align:middle line:84%
the integral over sets
that have positive measure.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:29.740 align:middle line:84%
So no matter what
function I take,

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.860 align:middle line:84%
the integral over a
set of measure 0 is 0.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.070 align:middle line:84%
OK, this is kind of akin
to, in Riemann integration,

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.520 align:middle line:84%
the integral over
a point being 0.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:41.590 align:middle line:84%
But now we have more
interesting sets

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:43.760 align:middle line:84%
of measure 0 other
than just a point.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:44.930 align:middle line:90%
So what's the proof of this?

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:46.513 align:middle line:84%
Well, we don't have
much to go off of.

00:06:46.513 --> 00:06:47.900 align:middle line:90%
We just have the definition.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.360 align:middle line:90%
So let's use the definition.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:07:16.390 align:middle line:84%
Let phi be simple with this
sort of canonical representation

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:18.460 align:middle line:84%
with the less than
or equal to f.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:21.160 align:middle line:84%
So what I'd like to show is
that the integral of phi is 0.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.590 align:middle line:84%
But this is-- and
therefore, the integral

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.440 align:middle line:84%
of f, which is the sup over
all of these integrals,

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:27.790 align:middle line:90%
is therefore 0.

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:36.980 align:middle line:84%
But this is clear because since
all of these Aj's are subsets

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:44.520 align:middle line:84%
of E, which is a
set of measure 0,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:49.380 align:middle line:84%
this implies the measure
of Aj equals 0 for all j.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:56.890 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
integral of phi over E,

00:07:56.890 --> 00:08:02.680 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to Aj
measure of Aj equals 0.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:05.440 align:middle line:84%
And thus, the
integral of f, which

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.065 align:middle line:84%
is the sup over all of these,
is just a sup of 0 then.

00:08:08.065 --> 00:08:13.590 align:middle line:90%


00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.990 align:middle line:84%
So I guess I should have
started this proof off with let

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:20.550 align:middle line:84%
f be an L plus of E.
So I left that off,

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:27.210 align:middle line:84%
but you know what I
was doing, hopefully.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:32.760 align:middle line:84%
OK, so it's only interesting
to take the integral

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:34.290 align:middle line:90%
over sets of positive measure.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:37.289 align:middle line:90%


00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:41.309 align:middle line:84%
Now, we have a few
facts which carry over.

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:47.722 align:middle line:90%


00:08:47.722 --> 00:08:48.680 align:middle line:90%
Well, not really carry.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.270 align:middle line:84%
Well, one of them
carries over from what

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:52.853 align:middle line:84%
we did for simple functions and
the others just kind of follow

00:08:52.853 --> 00:08:54.920 align:middle line:90%
from the definition.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.060 align:middle line:84%
And it'll be an exercise
in the assignment,

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.850 align:middle line:90%
so it's the following.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:20.060 align:middle line:84%
If phi is-- then
the two definitions

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:24.225 align:middle line:90%
of the integral of phi agree.

00:09:24.225 --> 00:09:26.750 align:middle line:90%


00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:31.730 align:middle line:84%
And so I'll say exactly what
I mean in just a second.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:50.370 align:middle line:84%
Two, f and g are in L plus of E.
c is a non-negative real number

00:09:50.370 --> 00:10:01.770 align:middle line:84%
and f is less than or equal to
g on E. Then a couple of things.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.920 align:middle line:84%
The integral of c
times f is equal to c

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:10.690 align:middle line:84%
times the integral
of f and the integral

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:17.500 align:middle line:84%
over E of f is less than or
equal to the integral of g

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:20.290 align:middle line:90%
over E.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:23.080 align:middle line:84%
And one final property
is the following.

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:25.710 align:middle line:90%


00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:35.120 align:middle line:84%
If f is a measurable
function on E

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:43.300 align:middle line:84%
and f is a measurable
subset of E,

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:47.510 align:middle line:84%
then I can integrate f also on
capital F. So this is in the,

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:52.210 align:middle line:84%
I think, maybe assignment 5
that if I have a function which

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.490 align:middle line:84%
is measurable on
a set and I take

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:57.950 align:middle line:84%
any subset of that measurable
set, which is measurable,

00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:01.120 align:middle line:84%
then f restricted to this
set is also measurable.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:03.160 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm about
to say makes sense.

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:10.090 align:middle line:84%
Then the integral of
little f over capital F

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:15.820 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral of E
of little f times the indicator

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:24.810 align:middle line:84%
function of f, which is less
than or equal to the integral

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.720 align:middle line:90%
over E of little f.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:31.255 align:middle line:90%


00:11:31.255 --> 00:11:36.740 align:middle line:84%
So I think the rest of the
statements, statements 2 and 3

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:39.530 align:middle line:90%
are completely unambiguous.

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:43.670 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you were wondering
what exactly did I mean by 1.

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:47.750 align:middle line:84%
So we had kind of two
definitions of the integral

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:49.010 align:middle line:90%
of a simple function, right?

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:55.070 align:middle line:84%
We defined it first as the
sum of the coefficients

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:57.860 align:middle line:90%
times the measure of these sets.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:01.460 align:middle line:84%
And then we also have
a second definition

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:04.250 align:middle line:90%
because a simple function--

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:08.160 align:middle line:84%
a measurable non-negative simple
function is also in this set.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.750 align:middle line:84%
So I should have that
what's here should be

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:16.460 align:middle line:90%
equal to what's on the right.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:24.600 align:middle line:84%
So statement 1 is the
statement that what's

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:27.570 align:middle line:84%
underlined three
times is equal to what

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:31.213 align:middle line:90%
was underlined four times.

00:12:31.213 --> 00:12:32.130 align:middle line:90%
That's a lot of lines.

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:36.520 align:middle line:90%


00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:43.330 align:middle line:84%
All right, so this will be a
fairly straightforward exercise

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:44.530 align:middle line:90%
just using the definitions.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:47.230 align:middle line:84%
And also, what we did
for simple functions.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:49.830 align:middle line:90%


00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:55.870 align:middle line:84%
One consequence of this
theorem and the one before it

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:56.830 align:middle line:90%
is the following.

00:12:56.830 --> 00:13:00.700 align:middle line:84%
Is that I can relax
this condition here

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:03.670 align:middle line:84%
in 2 to an almost
everywhere statement.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:06.280 align:middle line:90%


00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:16.126 align:middle line:84%
So if f and g are non-negative
measurable functions,

00:13:16.126 --> 00:13:24.090 align:middle line:84%
and f is less than or equal
to g almost everywhere on E,

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:30.810 align:middle line:84%
then the integral of
E of f is less than

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:35.580 align:middle line:90%
or equal to the integral of g.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:42.760 align:middle line:90%


00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:45.400 align:middle line:84%
So let's write
the proof of this.

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:47.920 align:middle line:90%


00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:56.520 align:middle line:84%
So let f be the set of
all x's and E such that

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:59.325 align:middle line:84%
f of x is less than
or equal to g of x.

00:13:59.325 --> 00:14:03.810 align:middle line:90%


00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:09.390 align:middle line:84%
So it's not difficult to realize
that this is a measurable set.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:15.990 align:middle line:84%
This is, if you'd
like, f minus g.

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:19.340 align:middle line:90%
The inverse image of--

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:20.435 align:middle line:90%
or let's do it this way.

00:14:20.435 --> 00:14:23.730 align:middle line:90%


00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:32.070 align:middle line:84%
Write this as g minus f
inverse image of 0 infinity.

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:35.970 align:middle line:90%


00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:38.040 align:middle line:84%
And since g and f are
measurable functions,

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:44.730 align:middle line:84%
their difference is
measurable, non-negative.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:48.296 align:middle line:90%
So this is always non-negative.

00:14:48.296 --> 00:14:52.278 align:middle line:84%
OK, so maybe there's
a small issue

00:14:52.278 --> 00:14:54.320 align:middle line:84%
with what happens at
infinity, but you're dealing

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:55.350 align:middle line:90%
with that in the assignment.

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:57.267 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just going to
erase this from the board

00:14:57.267 --> 00:14:59.060 align:middle line:84%
and you'll just have
to accept that this

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:02.510 align:middle line:84%
is measurable under the
wisdom that I gave you

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.210 align:middle line:84%
that if you can write it down,
typically it's measurable.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:08.090 align:middle line:90%


00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:13.440 align:middle line:84%
And what is the measure
of the complement

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:17.575 align:middle line:84%
is 0 because this is supposed
to hold almost everywhere.

00:15:17.575 --> 00:15:48.240 align:middle line:90%


00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.930 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I left off the fact,
which strictly speaking,

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:54.765 align:middle line:90%
we might need for this, but.

00:15:54.765 --> 00:16:06.080 align:middle line:90%


00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:10.250 align:middle line:90%
OK, so this is also an exercise.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:16.730 align:middle line:90%


00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:23.870 align:middle line:84%
So then the integral
of little f over E,

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:28.760 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to the
integral over E of f union, f

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:30.020 align:middle line:90%
complement here--

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:33.110 align:middle line:90%


00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:38.180 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to the
integral over f, little f,

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:43.670 align:middle line:84%
plus the integral over
f complement of little f

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:49.680 align:middle line:84%
So strictly speaking I didn't
write why this is true down.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:53.070 align:middle line:84%
But let's think about
it just for a moment.

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:54.620 align:middle line:84%
So these are two
disjoint subsets

00:16:54.620 --> 00:17:00.990 align:middle line:84%
that make up E. Why is this
going to be equal to this?

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:04.109 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's true for
simple functions.

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:06.980 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, if I make this
statement and assume phi--

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:11.270 align:middle line:84%
I mean, f is simple,
then it's not

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:14.130 align:middle line:84%
hard to convince yourself
that this is true.

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:16.040 align:middle line:84%
And if it's true for
simple functions,

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:20.810 align:middle line:84%
then from how we've defined
the integral as to be this sup,

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:24.380 align:middle line:84%
it will carry over to general,
non-negative measurable

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:25.349 align:middle line:90%
functions.

00:17:25.349 --> 00:17:30.100 align:middle line:84%
So let's just accept this and
you can prove it on your own.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:32.540 align:middle line:90%
It's not difficult.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:38.680 align:middle line:84%
But since we have
this and f complement

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:45.495 align:middle line:84%
is a set of measure 0, this
is the integral of little f.

00:17:45.495 --> 00:17:50.460 align:middle line:90%


00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:52.370 align:middle line:90%
All right, since this is 0.

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:55.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, on capital F, little f
is less than or equal to g.

00:17:55.970 --> 00:17:58.190 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than or
equal to the integral

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:01.400 align:middle line:90%
of g over capital F.

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:03.530 align:middle line:84%
And just going
backwards, this is

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:10.490 align:middle line:84%
equal to the integral of
fg plus f complement, which

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:20.070 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
integral of g, G. So

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:28.560 align:middle line:84%
modulo this equality, which
I leave to you to fill in,

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:29.370 align:middle line:90%
proves the theorem.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:39.408 align:middle line:90%


00:18:39.408 --> 00:18:42.230 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now we have
the definition

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:46.560 align:middle line:84%
of Lebesgue integral for
non-negative measurable

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:47.060 align:middle line:90%
functions.

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:48.920 align:middle line:90%
We have some properties of it.

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:50.960 align:middle line:84%
What's kind of
missing from this list

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:56.390 align:middle line:84%
that I've given so far is that
linearity, I guess, right?

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:59.000 align:middle line:84%
The integral of the sum of
two non-negative measurable

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:01.190 align:middle line:84%
functions is equal to
the sum of the integrals.

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:04.280 align:middle line:84%
We had that for
simple functions.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:13.520 align:middle line:84%
How do we get that for general
non-negative measurable

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:14.430 align:middle line:90%
functions?

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:21.020 align:middle line:90%


00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:23.310 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what I'm
about to prove is not

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:27.690 align:middle line:84%
just a tool for proving that
but is one of the big three

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:29.310 align:middle line:84%
convergence theorems
that you find

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:31.410 align:middle line:84%
in Lebesgue measure in
integration or Lebesgue

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.830 align:middle line:84%
integration, which
is the following--

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:40.090 align:middle line:90%


00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:51.290 align:middle line:84%
monotone convergence
theorem, which

00:19:51.290 --> 00:20:03.600 align:middle line:84%
is the following that
if fn is a sequence

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:07.110 align:middle line:84%
of non-negative
measurable functions

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:11.280 align:middle line:84%
such that f1 is less
than or equal to f2

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:18.050 align:middle line:84%
is less than or
equal to f3 on E.

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:21.742 align:middle line:84%
So pointwise they
are increasing.

00:20:21.742 --> 00:20:24.160 align:middle line:90%
The sequence is increasing.

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:30.340 align:middle line:84%
And there exists a
function f so that fn

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:37.380 align:middle line:84%
goes to f pointwise on
E. So let me just recall,

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:44.070 align:middle line:84%
this means for all x and E
limit as n goes to infinity

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:47.205 align:middle line:90%
of fn of x equals f of x.

00:20:47.205 --> 00:20:51.320 align:middle line:90%


00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:55.010 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, f is going
to be a non-negative measurable

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:57.140 align:middle line:84%
function because remember,
the pointwise limit

00:20:57.140 --> 00:21:00.580 align:middle line:84%
of measurable functions
is measurable,

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:03.910 align:middle line:84%
so what I'm about
to say makes sense.

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:16.700 align:middle line:84%
Then the integrals converge
to the integrals of the limit.

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:21.880 align:middle line:84%
So the limit of the integrals
is the integral of the limits.

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:25.410 align:middle line:84%
So this is a much stronger
statement than anything

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:28.650 align:middle line:84%
you come across in
Riemann integration.

00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:32.130 align:middle line:84%
Riemann integration usually
requires uniform convergence

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:37.350 align:middle line:84%
while here, at least
for monotone sequences,

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:39.030 align:middle line:84%
we just need
pointwise convergence.

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:43.950 align:middle line:84%
So I think there is a
version of this theorem

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:48.960 align:middle line:84%
that one could state
for Riemann integration.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:51.600 align:middle line:84%
But still, just
on the face of it,

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:56.310 align:middle line:84%
you have a pointwise
statement implying

00:21:56.310 --> 00:21:57.670 align:middle line:90%
convergence of integrals.

00:21:57.670 --> 00:22:03.150 align:middle line:84%
So that should immediately
kind of suggest to you

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:05.760 align:middle line:84%
that what we've built up,
this Lebesgue integration,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:07.860 align:middle line:84%
is much more powerful
than Riemann integration.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:11.625 align:middle line:90%
So let's prove the theorem.

00:22:11.625 --> 00:22:15.300 align:middle line:90%


00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:21.330 align:middle line:84%
So since f1 is less
than or equal to f2

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:25.290 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to f3 and so on,

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:28.410 align:middle line:84%
this implies the
integral of E of f1

00:22:28.410 --> 00:22:32.880 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
integral of f2 and so on.

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:35.680 align:middle line:90%


00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:38.040 align:middle line:90%
And what else?

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:49.930 align:middle line:84%
So-- which implies
that the limit as n

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:58.820 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of
the integrals of fn

00:22:58.820 --> 00:23:03.710 align:middle line:90%
exists in 0 infinity.

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:07.570 align:middle line:84%
So this is a non-negative
increasing sequence

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:09.220 align:middle line:90%
of real numbers now.

00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:12.230 align:middle line:84%
The integral over E of
f1, that's a real number.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:13.180 align:middle line:90%
This is a real number.

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:14.800 align:middle line:84%
All of these are
non-negative numbers

00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:17.690 align:middle line:84%
because this is a sup
over non-negative numbers.

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:20.870 align:middle line:84%
So I have an increasing sequence
of non-negative numbers.

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:23.560 align:middle line:84%
So that either has a
limit, a finite limit,

00:23:23.560 --> 00:23:25.610 align:middle line:90%
or it must go to infinity.

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:28.090 align:middle line:84%
That's not difficult
to prove knowing what

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:30.790 align:middle line:90%
from basic analysis.

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:34.010 align:middle line:90%


00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:53.580 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, since their pointwise
increasing and converging

00:23:53.580 --> 00:24:00.640 align:middle line:84%
for all x, this
implies that we still

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:04.040 align:middle line:84%
have f1 is less than or
equal to f3 and so on.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:07.420 align:middle line:90%
But they all sit below f.

00:24:07.420 --> 00:24:11.170 align:middle line:84%
So for each x-- this
is a f1 of x, f2 of x,

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:14.230 align:middle line:84%
f3 of x and so on this is an
increasing sequence of real

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:18.700 align:middle line:84%
numbers converging to f of x,
which is either a finite number

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:20.880 align:middle line:90%
or infinity. .

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:25.790 align:middle line:84%
So these numbers are
increasing to this limit,

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:29.285 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, they must
always sit below the limit.

00:24:29.285 --> 00:24:35.150 align:middle line:90%


00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:40.490 align:middle line:84%
And since all of these
functions sit below f,

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:49.680 align:middle line:84%
this implies that for all
n, the integral of fn over E

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:52.890 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the integral of f.

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:55.800 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
limit, which we

00:24:55.800 --> 00:25:02.260 align:middle line:84%
know exists as either a
finite number or infinity,

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:07.420 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the integral of f.

00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:10.900 align:middle line:84%
So just based on
the assumptions,

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:15.660 align:middle line:84%
we immediately get that one of
these quantities that we want

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:17.410 align:middle line:84%
to show is equal to
the other is less than

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:21.890 align:middle line:90%
or equal to the other quantity.

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:24.700 align:middle line:90%
So standard trick of analysis.

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:26.587 align:middle line:84%
If you get kind of for
free one quantity is

00:25:26.587 --> 00:25:29.170 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal to the other
quantity that you want to show

00:25:29.170 --> 00:25:32.270 align:middle line:84%
are equal, let's try and
go the reverse direction.

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:44.740 align:middle line:84%
So now we show that the
integral of f over E

00:25:44.740 --> 00:25:49.660 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
the integral of the limit

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:55.940 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity of
the integral over E of fn.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:57.315 align:middle line:84%
And therefore,
the two are equal.

00:25:57.315 --> 00:26:01.620 align:middle line:90%


00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:03.128 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:26:03.128 --> 00:26:04.670 align:middle line:84%
Now to show this,
we're going to show

00:26:04.670 --> 00:26:07.400 align:middle line:84%
that for every simple
function less than

00:26:07.400 --> 00:26:09.320 align:middle line:84%
or equal to f, the
integral of that

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:10.895 align:middle line:90%
simple function sits below this.

00:26:10.895 --> 00:26:13.620 align:middle line:90%


00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.830 align:middle line:84%
Now, we know these fn's
so here's the game plan.

00:26:15.830 --> 00:26:18.230 align:middle line:84%
We know the fn's
are increasing to f.

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:22.460 align:middle line:84%
So if I take a simple function
less than or equal to f--

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:26.030 align:middle line:84%
if the simple function is
less than f, then eventually,

00:26:26.030 --> 00:26:28.250 align:middle line:84%
fn is going to
pass it up, right?

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:30.950 align:middle line:84%
Because the fn's are increasing
to f and the simple function

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:32.600 align:middle line:90%
sits below f.

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:35.538 align:middle line:84%
And therefore,
eventually, we should

00:26:35.538 --> 00:26:37.580 align:middle line:84%
have this is bigger than
or equal to the integral

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:38.580 align:middle line:90%
of that simple function.

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:41.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're only requiring
the simple function

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:44.180 align:middle line:84%
to be less than or equal to
f so we'll give ourselves

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:47.180 align:middle line:84%
a little bit of room and then
send that bit of room to 0.

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:57.225 align:middle line:84%
So let phi be a non-negative
simple function.

00:26:57.225 --> 00:27:02.890 align:middle line:90%


00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:17.160 align:middle line:84%
Phi equals sum j equals 1 to
n Aj chi Aj with phi less than

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:17.850 align:middle line:90%
or equal to f.

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:19.890 align:middle line:84%
And our goal is to show
that the integral of phi

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:21.330 align:middle line:84%
is less than or
equal to the limit

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:22.860 align:middle line:90%
as n goes to infinity of fn.

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:27.520 align:middle line:90%


00:27:27.520 --> 00:27:31.500 align:middle line:84%
So here's that little bit
of room I was referring to.

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:35.160 align:middle line:84%
Let epsilon be a small
number between 0 and 1.

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:44.490 align:middle line:84%
And let En be the
set of all x's and E

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:50.400 align:middle line:84%
such that fn of x is
greater than or equal to 1

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:53.770 align:middle line:90%
minus epsilon f of x--

00:27:53.770 --> 00:27:54.610 align:middle line:90%
I mean, phi of x.

00:27:54.610 --> 00:28:02.130 align:middle line:90%


00:28:02.130 --> 00:28:14.200 align:middle line:84%
So note for all x in E, 1 minus
epsilon times phi of x, this

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:16.660 align:middle line:84%
is strictly less
than now f of x.

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:19.900 align:middle line:84%
So we had phi of x is less
than or equal to f of x.

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:23.890 align:middle line:84%
But now, if I multiply this by
a small number that's close to--

00:28:23.890 --> 00:28:27.160 align:middle line:84%
or a number that's
slightly less than 1,

00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:29.170 align:middle line:84%
then I will have
strict inequality.

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:37.870 align:middle line:90%


00:28:37.870 --> 00:28:51.650 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, then since
for all x and E I have a--

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:59.640 align:middle line:90%


00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:01.950 align:middle line:90%
equals f of x.

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:05.340 align:middle line:84%
This implies that
every x must eventually

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:07.650 align:middle line:84%
lie in one of these
E sub fn's right?

00:29:07.650 --> 00:29:11.220 align:middle line:84%
Because 1 minus epsilon phi
of x is less than f of x.

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:14.610 align:middle line:84%
The fn's are approaching
f of x so it must pass up

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:17.130 align:middle line:84%
this value at some
point in its quest

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:20.910 align:middle line:84%
to get to f of x, or at
least close to f of x.

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:27.950 align:middle line:84%
So simply from this fact, this
implies that the union over n

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:33.693 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to infinity
of the En's gives me E.

00:29:33.693 --> 00:29:34.610 align:middle line:90%
Let me highlight this.

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:43.640 align:middle line:90%


00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:48.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, since these
functions are increasing--

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:51.020 align:middle line:84%
I should say, they're
pointwise increasing.

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:52.820 align:middle line:84%
Not that they are
increasing functions,

00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:54.620 align:middle line:84%
but they are
pointwise increasing.

00:29:54.620 --> 00:29:58.550 align:middle line:84%
So f1 is less than or equal to
f2 is less than or equal to f3

00:29:58.550 --> 00:29:59.840 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:04.640 align:middle line:84%
This implies that E1
is contained in E2

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:08.000 align:middle line:90%
is contained in E3 and so on.

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:11.790 align:middle line:84%
If I have some n so
that x is in this set--

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:13.760 align:middle line:84%
so fn of x is bigger
than or equal to 1

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:15.980 align:middle line:90%
minus epsilon phi of x--

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:19.053 align:middle line:84%
then f of n plus 1 of x is
bigger than or equal to fn

00:30:19.053 --> 00:30:20.720 align:middle line:84%
of x, which is bigger
than or equal to 1

00:30:20.720 --> 00:30:22.280 align:middle line:90%
minus epsilon phi of x.

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:27.480 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, that
x is then En plus 1.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:33.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is not only a sequence
of sets whose union gives me E,

00:30:33.630 --> 00:30:37.270 align:middle line:84%
they're an increasing
sequence of sets.

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:39.250 align:middle line:84%
Increasing in the
sense of inclusion.

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:42.830 align:middle line:90%


00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:43.330 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:53.450 align:middle line:90%


00:30:53.450 --> 00:30:58.250 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're going to use these
two highlighted things in just

00:30:58.250 --> 00:31:03.360 align:middle line:84%
a minute, along with
continuity of Lebesgue measure

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.500 align:middle line:90%
to get what we want.

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:16.610 align:middle line:90%
So we have the integral of E fn.

00:31:16.610 --> 00:31:20.120 align:middle line:84%
This is less than or equal
to the integral of fn

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:24.080 align:middle line:90%
over a smaller set, so E sub n.

00:31:24.080 --> 00:31:25.940 align:middle line:90%
Now, on E sub n--

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:27.500 align:middle line:84%
remember, the E
sub n's are defined

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:29.990 align:middle line:84%
as where fn is bigger
than or equal to 1

00:31:29.990 --> 00:31:32.000 align:middle line:90%
minus epsilon phi of x.

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:37.175 align:middle line:84%
So this is bigger than or
equal to E sub n 1 minus phi--

00:31:37.175 --> 00:31:40.730 align:middle line:90%


00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:42.470 align:middle line:90%
sorry, what am I doing?

00:31:42.470 --> 00:31:50.080 align:middle line:84%
Minus epsilon phi, which
is equal to 1 minus

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:54.490 align:middle line:84%
epsilon times the integral
of E sub n over phi.

00:31:54.490 --> 00:31:59.860 align:middle line:84%
And this is, by
definition, equal to a sum

00:31:59.860 --> 00:32:02.110 align:middle line:90%
from j equals 1 to n.

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:05.380 align:middle line:84%
Over the set E sub n, I
get integral Aj measure

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:09.460 align:middle line:90%
of Aj intersect E sub n.

00:32:09.460 --> 00:32:15.940 align:middle line:84%
And I made a small
notational error.

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:22.650 align:middle line:84%
Let's change this
into an m since we

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:24.570 align:middle line:90%
have n already denoting the--

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:28.690 align:middle line:90%


00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:31.610 align:middle line:84%
indexing the functions, we do
not want this n right here.

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:32.590 align:middle line:90%
So that should be an m.

00:32:32.590 --> 00:32:34.360 align:middle line:84%
It's just a fixed
finite number depending

00:32:34.360 --> 00:32:35.318 align:middle line:90%
on the simple function.

00:32:35.318 --> 00:32:38.100 align:middle line:90%


00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:41.520 align:middle line:90%
So I have this for all n.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:46.640 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
limit as n goes

00:32:46.640 --> 00:32:49.610 align:middle line:84%
to infinity of the
integral over E

00:32:49.610 --> 00:32:55.070 align:middle line:84%
of f sub n is less than or
equal to the limit as n goes

00:32:55.070 --> 00:32:59.540 align:middle line:84%
to infinity of 1 minus
epsilon times sum from j

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:09.130 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to m Aj
measure of Aj E sub n.

00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:16.120 align:middle line:84%
Now, the E sub n's are
increasing to E, and therefore,

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:18.430 align:middle line:90%
for each fixed j--

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:22.390 align:middle line:84%
so in fact, let's
pause on this real

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:28.470 align:middle line:84%
quick and come
back to this thing.

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:30.600 align:middle line:84%
We're going to eventually
take the limit as n goes

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:32.140 align:middle line:90%
to infinity of this quantity.

00:33:32.140 --> 00:33:34.170 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at
what this does as n

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:39.480 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity since by those
two things that I highlighted,

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:45.900 align:middle line:84%
that En's are increasing subsets
of E whose union gives me E,

00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:47.010 align:middle line:90%
I get--

00:33:47.010 --> 00:34:00.730 align:middle line:84%
since E1 Aj is a subset of
E2 Aj subset of E3 and so on.

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:07.690 align:middle line:84%
And the union n equals 1 to
infinity of E sub n intersect

00:34:07.690 --> 00:34:16.380 align:middle line:84%
Aj equals Aj, because
then this is just

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:19.409 align:middle line:84%
going to be equal to the union
of the E sub n's intersect Aj.

00:34:19.409 --> 00:34:22.770 align:middle line:84%
That just gives me E intersect
Aj, which is just Aj.

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:29.204 align:middle line:84%
We get by the continuity
of Lebesgue measure--

00:34:29.204 --> 00:34:31.110 align:middle line:90%
this implies for all j--

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:39.210 align:middle line:84%
that the limit as n goes to
infinity of the measure of Aj

00:34:39.210 --> 00:34:49.719 align:middle line:84%
intersect En is equal
to the measure of Aj,

00:34:49.719 --> 00:35:02.500 align:middle line:84%
which, as we just said, this
En intersect Aj, which--

00:35:02.500 --> 00:35:07.240 align:middle line:84%
remember, this set is equal to
Aj, so this is measure of Aj.

00:35:07.240 --> 00:35:12.160 align:middle line:84%
So from the two yellow
boxes we had before,

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:14.470 align:middle line:90%
we get this useful one.

00:35:14.470 --> 00:35:16.120 align:middle line:84%
For all j, we have
the limit as n

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:19.720 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of the
measure of Aj intersect En is

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:22.120 align:middle line:90%
equal to the measure of Aj.

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:27.250 align:middle line:84%
So now, we'll stick this
into this inequality

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:29.390 align:middle line:90%
after we take the limit.

00:35:29.390 --> 00:35:32.680 align:middle line:84%
So I got ahead of
myself a minute ago.

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:35.770 align:middle line:84%
Thus, limit as n
goes to infinity

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:39.700 align:middle line:84%
of the integral
over E of fn, this

00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:45.370 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than or
equal to limit as n

00:35:45.370 --> 00:35:51.800 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of
1 minus epsilon m

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:59.600 align:middle line:84%
now Aj measure of
Aj intersect En.

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:03.440 align:middle line:84%
Now, these numbers
here all converge to--

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:05.930 align:middle line:84%
each of these numbers
here for each j

00:36:05.930 --> 00:36:09.950 align:middle line:84%
converges as n goes to infinity
to the measure of A sub j.

00:36:09.950 --> 00:36:14.420 align:middle line:84%
So the limit is then
equal to 1 minus epsilon

00:36:14.420 --> 00:36:18.080 align:middle line:84%
times the integral j
equals 1 to m times--

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:22.166 align:middle line:90%


00:36:22.166 --> 00:36:24.400 align:middle line:84%
what am I writing
the integral for?

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:28.000 align:middle line:90%
Aj measure of Aj.

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:32.200 align:middle line:84%
And this is just equal
to 1 minus integral,

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:36.290 align:middle line:84%
by definition, the
integral of phi.

00:36:36.290 --> 00:36:41.780 align:middle line:84%
So I've shown that for all
epsilon between 0 and 1,

00:36:41.780 --> 00:36:54.680 align:middle line:84%
this 1 minus epsilon times the
integral of phi is less than

00:36:54.680 --> 00:37:01.370 align:middle line:84%
or equal to this number over
here, which may be infinite,

00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:02.090 align:middle line:90%
may be finite.

00:37:02.090 --> 00:37:05.590 align:middle line:90%


00:37:05.590 --> 00:37:07.180 align:middle line:84%
And since this hold
for all epsilon,

00:37:07.180 --> 00:37:08.485 align:middle line:90%
I can send epsilon to 0.

00:37:08.485 --> 00:37:19.550 align:middle line:90%


00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:23.750 align:middle line:84%
So I have this inequality
between now fixed things

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:27.470 align:middle line:84%
along with an epsilon here,
so I can send epsilon to 0

00:37:27.470 --> 00:37:31.400 align:middle line:84%
and I get the integral of phi is
less than or equal to the limit

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:35.775 align:middle line:90%
as n goes to infinity of fn.

00:37:35.775 --> 00:37:40.200 align:middle line:90%


00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:43.320 align:middle line:84%
And since phi is an arbitrary
simple function that's less

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:46.200 align:middle line:84%
than or equal to f, the sup
over all of these-- which is,

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:49.850 align:middle line:84%
by definition, the
integral of f--

00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:59.340 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
the limit of the integrals.

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:07.310 align:middle line:90%


00:38:07.310 --> 00:38:07.823 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:38:07.823 --> 00:38:08.990 align:middle line:90%
That's the end of the proof.

00:38:08.990 --> 00:38:14.300 align:middle line:90%


00:38:14.300 --> 00:38:28.570 align:middle line:84%
So monotone convergence
theorem, a very useful theorem,

00:38:28.570 --> 00:38:31.180 align:middle line:84%
important theorem
throughout all of this.

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:37.340 align:middle line:90%


00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:43.990 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's get a few
applications from this.

00:38:43.990 --> 00:38:46.870 align:middle line:84%
So this first one
is kind of a way

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:48.667 align:middle line:84%
how would you evaluate
now this integral?

00:38:48.667 --> 00:38:50.500 align:middle line:84%
Remember, the integral,
which I just erased,

00:38:50.500 --> 00:38:53.620 align:middle line:84%
is defined as the sup over all
integrals of simple functions.

00:38:53.620 --> 00:38:56.740 align:middle line:84%
So in order to actually
compute the integral

00:38:56.740 --> 00:38:59.740 align:middle line:84%
of a non-negative
measurable function,

00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:02.320 align:middle line:84%
I would have to find
out the integral

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:04.810 align:middle line:84%
of every simple function
less than or equal to it

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:06.610 align:middle line:84%
and take the sup over
that whole set, which

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:12.010 align:middle line:84%
is kind of a useless or
impossible way of computing

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:14.290 align:middle line:90%
the integral.

00:39:14.290 --> 00:39:18.520 align:middle line:84%
It's similar to when you come
across Riemann integration

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:20.290 align:middle line:84%
and the Riemann
integral is defined

00:39:20.290 --> 00:39:24.520 align:middle line:90%
as the limit of Riemann sums.

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:28.480 align:middle line:84%
Nobody-- you can compute
maybe three integrals just

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:29.350 align:middle line:90%
from Riemann sum.

00:39:29.350 --> 00:39:32.377 align:middle line:84%
So we need a more
efficient way of being

00:39:32.377 --> 00:39:33.960 align:middle line:84%
able to compute the
Lebesgue integral,

00:39:33.960 --> 00:39:35.640 align:middle line:84%
and the monotone
convergence theorem

00:39:35.640 --> 00:39:38.350 align:middle line:90%
gives us that kind of for free.

00:39:38.350 --> 00:39:45.060 align:middle line:84%
So we have the
following, if f is

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:51.445 align:middle line:84%
a non-negative measurable
function and phi

00:39:51.445 --> 00:40:07.540 align:middle line:84%
n is a sequence of simple
functions, which are all

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:21.610 align:middle line:84%
non-negative and pointwise
increasing and converging

00:40:21.610 --> 00:40:33.240 align:middle line:84%
pointwise to f then the
integral over E of f

00:40:33.240 --> 00:40:36.990 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the limit
as n goes to infinity

00:40:36.990 --> 00:40:42.570 align:middle line:84%
of the integral of
the simple functions.

00:40:42.570 --> 00:40:45.760 align:middle line:84%
So back when we discussed
measurable functions,

00:40:45.760 --> 00:40:48.510 align:middle line:84%
we actually constructed such
a sequence of simple functions

00:40:48.510 --> 00:40:51.130 align:middle line:84%
that satisfies the
hypotheses of this theorem.

00:40:51.130 --> 00:40:52.650 align:middle line:84%
So this is not a
vacuous theorem.

00:40:52.650 --> 00:40:54.480 align:middle line:84%
But this theorem
tells you that if you

00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:56.130 align:middle line:84%
want to compute
the integral of f,

00:40:56.130 --> 00:40:59.970 align:middle line:84%
just take any sequence of
simple functions increasing up

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:02.340 align:middle line:84%
to f and compute the
limit of the integral.

00:41:02.340 --> 00:41:04.965 align:middle line:84%
And that'll give you
the integral of f.

00:41:04.965 --> 00:41:07.590 align:middle line:90%


00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:09.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, there's just this--
this follows immediately

00:41:09.660 --> 00:41:11.243 align:middle line:84%
from the monotone
convergence theorem.

00:41:11.243 --> 00:41:15.600 align:middle line:84%
I have the taking fn is
equal to the phi n's.

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:18.000 align:middle line:84%
So there's no proof
to go with that.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:22.320 align:middle line:90%


00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:26.260 align:middle line:84%
The next theorem is
linearity of the integral.

00:41:26.260 --> 00:41:32.220 align:middle line:84%
So if f and g are two
non-negative measurable

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:39.020 align:middle line:84%
functions, then the
integral of f plus g

00:41:39.020 --> 00:41:45.515 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral of
f plus the integral of g.

00:41:45.515 --> 00:41:48.770 align:middle line:90%


00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:53.000 align:middle line:84%
Now, note there's no
ambiguity with how to define,

00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:54.400 align:middle line:90%
so we kind of had a--

00:41:54.400 --> 00:41:58.550 align:middle line:84%
there's some touchy business
about adding and subtracting

00:41:58.550 --> 00:42:00.740 align:middle line:84%
two extended real valued
measurable functions,

00:42:00.740 --> 00:42:02.448 align:middle line:84%
but there's none of
that here since these

00:42:02.448 --> 00:42:06.480 align:middle line:84%
are both non-negative measurable
extended real valued functions.

00:42:06.480 --> 00:42:08.240 align:middle line:84%
So this will always
only be of the form

00:42:08.240 --> 00:42:11.160 align:middle line:84%
infinity plus infinity, which
we define to be infinity.

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:14.300 align:middle line:84%
So just let me make
that small note.

00:42:14.300 --> 00:42:17.975 align:middle line:84%
So the integral is linear,
so what's the proof?

00:42:17.975 --> 00:42:22.370 align:middle line:90%


00:42:22.370 --> 00:42:42.400 align:middle line:84%
Let phi n and psi n be two
sequences of simple functions

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:46.070 align:middle line:84%
such that they're increasing
to f and g, respectively.

00:42:46.070 --> 00:42:50.740 align:middle line:84%
So 0 is less than
or equal to phi 1

00:42:50.740 --> 00:42:54.790 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to phi 2 and so on.

00:42:54.790 --> 00:43:02.050 align:middle line:84%
And phi n converges
to f pointwise on E.

00:43:02.050 --> 00:43:04.120 align:middle line:90%
OK, so I should have--

00:43:04.120 --> 00:43:07.030 align:middle line:84%
everything's happening
on this set E.

00:43:07.030 --> 00:43:08.425 align:middle line:90%
And the same for the psis.

00:43:08.425 --> 00:43:12.510 align:middle line:90%


00:43:12.510 --> 00:43:18.195 align:middle line:84%
And psi n converging
to g pointwise.

00:43:18.195 --> 00:43:25.270 align:middle line:90%


00:43:25.270 --> 00:43:29.740 align:middle line:84%
Then, if I take the sum of
these two simple functions

00:43:29.740 --> 00:43:31.210 align:middle line:84%
or sequences of
simple functions,

00:43:31.210 --> 00:43:38.580 align:middle line:84%
I get an increasing sequence
of simple functions.

00:43:38.580 --> 00:43:41.740 align:middle line:90%


00:43:41.740 --> 00:43:52.430 align:middle line:84%
And phi n plus psi n converges
to f plus g pointwise.

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:56.820 align:middle line:90%


00:43:56.820 --> 00:43:58.860 align:middle line:84%
And by this theorem that
followed immediately

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:05.730 align:middle line:84%
from the monotone
convergence theorem,

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:12.960 align:middle line:84%
I get that the integral
of f plus g over E-- this

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:17.790 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the limit as n
goes to infinity of integral

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:20.850 align:middle line:90%
of E of phi n plus psi n.

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:23.910 align:middle line:84%
And now, we've proved
linearity of the integral

00:44:23.910 --> 00:44:26.470 align:middle line:90%
for simple functions.

00:44:26.470 --> 00:44:29.850 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal
to the limit as n

00:44:29.850 --> 00:44:33.225 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of
the integral of E

00:44:33.225 --> 00:44:39.950 align:middle line:84%
of phi n plus the
integral of E of psi n.

00:44:39.950 --> 00:44:44.630 align:middle line:84%
And again, by the theorem that
I just stated a minute ago,

00:44:44.630 --> 00:44:47.030 align:middle line:84%
by the monotone convergence
theorem, this converges

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:49.310 align:middle line:84%
through the integral
of f, this converges

00:44:49.310 --> 00:44:50.510 align:middle line:90%
through the integral of g.

00:44:50.510 --> 00:44:59.950 align:middle line:84%
So the limit of the sum is the
sum of the limits, and I get g.

00:44:59.950 --> 00:45:08.505 align:middle line:90%


00:45:08.505 --> 00:45:10.970 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:45:10.970 --> 00:45:13.670 align:middle line:84%
Using the same kind of
argument, if you like--

00:45:13.670 --> 00:45:15.920 align:middle line:84%
except now not
for two functions,

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:17.760 align:middle line:90%
but for one function--

00:45:17.760 --> 00:45:21.730 align:middle line:84%
you can show that the
integral of a function

00:45:21.730 --> 00:45:24.793 align:middle line:84%
over a union of
two disjoint sets

00:45:24.793 --> 00:45:25.960 align:middle line:90%
is the sum of the integrals.

00:45:25.960 --> 00:45:29.000 align:middle line:84%
This is something
that I pointed to

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:31.840 align:middle line:84%
but didn't prove at the very
beginning of this lecture.

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:36.040 align:middle line:84%
But using that that is true
for simple functions and this

00:45:36.040 --> 00:45:38.240 align:middle line:84%
argument using the monotone
convergence theorem--

00:45:38.240 --> 00:45:41.440 align:middle line:84%
which didn't require what I had
proved earlier so this is not

00:45:41.440 --> 00:45:44.340 align:middle line:90%
a circular argument--

00:45:44.340 --> 00:45:46.950 align:middle line:84%
you can prove that the integral
of non-negative measurable

00:45:46.950 --> 00:45:49.650 align:middle line:84%
function over a union
of two disjoint sets

00:45:49.650 --> 00:45:53.340 align:middle line:84%
is the sum of the integrals,
one over the first set,

00:45:53.340 --> 00:45:54.600 align:middle line:90%
one over the second set.

00:45:54.600 --> 00:45:57.312 align:middle line:90%


00:45:57.312 --> 00:46:02.150 align:middle line:90%
All right, so that's cool.

00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:06.530 align:middle line:90%
This integral is linear over--

00:46:06.530 --> 00:46:10.940 align:middle line:84%
so the integral of the sum
of two measurable functions

00:46:10.940 --> 00:46:12.440 align:middle line:90%
is the sum of the integrals.

00:46:12.440 --> 00:46:15.860 align:middle line:84%
What's even better is that the
integral over an infinite sum

00:46:15.860 --> 00:46:20.070 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the infinite
sum of the integrals.

00:46:20.070 --> 00:46:26.140 align:middle line:90%
So actually, let me state--

00:46:26.140 --> 00:46:27.550 align:middle line:90%
or let's just do this.

00:46:27.550 --> 00:46:31.564 align:middle line:90%


00:46:31.564 --> 00:46:41.990 align:middle line:84%
If fn is a sequence of
non-negative measurable

00:46:41.990 --> 00:46:50.200 align:middle line:84%
functions, then the
integral of the sum of E

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:56.600 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the infinite
sum of the integrals.

00:46:56.600 --> 00:47:01.550 align:middle line:90%


00:47:01.550 --> 00:47:05.090 align:middle line:84%
Well, first off, this is
a well-defined function

00:47:05.090 --> 00:47:07.190 align:middle line:84%
because it's a sum
of non-negative.

00:47:07.190 --> 00:47:09.860 align:middle line:84%
So pointwise for
each x, this is a sum

00:47:09.860 --> 00:47:11.820 align:middle line:90%
of non-negative real numbers.

00:47:11.820 --> 00:47:13.730 align:middle line:84%
So that's either going
to be a finite number

00:47:13.730 --> 00:47:16.650 align:middle line:84%
if this series converges or
it's going to be infinite,

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:17.150 align:middle line:90%
all right?

00:47:17.150 --> 00:47:20.600 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we're allowing
extended real value

00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:24.200 align:middle line:84%
non-negative measurable
functions in our framework

00:47:24.200 --> 00:47:25.370 align:middle line:90%
for now.

00:47:25.370 --> 00:47:27.450 align:middle line:90%
So this is meaningful.

00:47:27.450 --> 00:47:30.140 align:middle line:84%
And it's a measurable
function by stuff

00:47:30.140 --> 00:47:34.420 align:middle line:84%
we proved in the section
on measurable functions.

00:47:34.420 --> 00:47:38.685 align:middle line:84%
OK, so the proof
is pretty short.

00:47:38.685 --> 00:47:48.190 align:middle line:90%


00:47:48.190 --> 00:47:55.330 align:middle line:84%
By an induction argument
and the previous theorem

00:47:55.330 --> 00:48:07.730 align:middle line:84%
for the sum of two functions,
we have the statement

00:48:07.730 --> 00:48:12.170 align:middle line:84%
that for every fixed
natural number of capital

00:48:12.170 --> 00:48:16.400 align:middle line:84%
N, the integral of
the sum, n equals 1

00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:26.620 align:middle line:84%
to capital N fn E is
equal to the finite sum

00:48:26.620 --> 00:48:27.610 align:middle line:90%
of the integrals.

00:48:27.610 --> 00:48:34.180 align:middle line:90%


00:48:34.180 --> 00:48:37.360 align:middle line:84%
So if you can do
something for two,

00:48:37.360 --> 00:48:41.500 align:middle line:84%
usually you can do something
for n by an induction argument.

00:48:41.500 --> 00:48:44.260 align:middle line:84%
So I'll leave the
details of this to you

00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:47.298 align:middle line:84%
or you can just believe it based
on how many induction arguments

00:48:47.298 --> 00:48:48.340 align:middle line:90%
you've done in your life.

00:48:48.340 --> 00:48:51.060 align:middle line:90%


00:48:51.060 --> 00:48:53.460 align:middle line:90%
So we have this.

00:48:53.460 --> 00:49:04.060 align:middle line:84%
And so since n equals
1, 1 fn is less than

00:49:04.060 --> 00:49:07.720 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the sum from
n equals 1 to 2 of fn

00:49:07.720 --> 00:49:11.650 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
sum from n equals 1 to 3 fn.

00:49:11.650 --> 00:49:14.330 align:middle line:84%
Because these are all
non-negative functions,

00:49:14.330 --> 00:49:19.840 align:middle line:84%
so adding non-negative functions
to something only increases it.

00:49:19.840 --> 00:49:22.670 align:middle line:90%


00:49:22.670 --> 00:49:32.510 align:middle line:84%
And sum from n equals
1 to n converges

00:49:32.510 --> 00:49:45.700 align:middle line:84%
to pointwise simply by
defining this to be the limit

00:49:45.700 --> 00:49:49.090 align:middle line:84%
as capital N goes to infinity
of fn of x pointwise.

00:49:49.090 --> 00:49:55.123 align:middle line:90%


00:49:55.123 --> 00:49:57.040 align:middle line:84%
All right, since I have
these two things, then

00:49:57.040 --> 00:50:06.130 align:middle line:84%
by monotone convergence theorem,
I get that the integral of n

00:50:06.130 --> 00:50:11.770 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to infinity of fn of
E. this is equal to the limit

00:50:11.770 --> 00:50:18.340 align:middle line:84%
as capital N goes to infinity
of the integral of E sum from n

00:50:18.340 --> 00:50:27.740 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to capital N, which,
by what we have right here,

00:50:27.740 --> 00:50:32.720 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the limit as capital
N goes to infinity of-- now

00:50:32.720 --> 00:50:37.550 align:middle line:90%
the finite sum comes out.

00:50:37.550 --> 00:50:42.065 align:middle line:84%
And this is by definition
this infinite sum.

00:50:42.065 --> 00:50:45.490 align:middle line:90%


00:50:45.490 --> 00:50:53.830 align:middle line:84%
So for non-negative
measurable functions,

00:50:53.830 --> 00:50:57.940 align:middle line:84%
the integral of the sum is equal
to the sum of the integrals,

00:50:57.940 --> 00:50:59.440 align:middle line:90%
even for an infinite sum.

00:50:59.440 --> 00:51:05.930 align:middle line:90%


00:51:05.930 --> 00:51:11.090 align:middle line:84%
So again, this is simply false
for if I replace everything

00:51:11.090 --> 00:51:12.140 align:middle line:90%
by Riemann integration.

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:15.710 align:middle line:84%
Because, in fact, I can come up
with a sequence of functions,

00:51:15.710 --> 00:51:18.110 align:middle line:84%
fn, whose Riemann
integral is always 0,

00:51:18.110 --> 00:51:19.880 align:middle line:84%
but the sum is not
Riemann integrable.

00:51:19.880 --> 00:51:23.390 align:middle line:84%
Think of taking fn to
be the function, which

00:51:23.390 --> 00:51:27.815 align:middle line:90%
is 0 off of a rational number.

00:51:27.815 --> 00:51:31.130 align:middle line:90%


00:51:31.130 --> 00:51:36.870 align:middle line:84%
And then so first, enumerate
the rationals Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4,

00:51:36.870 --> 00:51:38.990 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:51:38.990 --> 00:51:41.630 align:middle line:84%
And take fn to be
the function that

00:51:41.630 --> 00:51:46.940 align:middle line:84%
is 0 when x is not equal to Qn
and 1 when x is equal to Qn.

00:51:46.940 --> 00:51:48.890 align:middle line:84%
Then the infinite
sum is just going

00:51:48.890 --> 00:51:53.000 align:middle line:84%
to be the indicator function
of the rational, say, in 0, 1.

00:51:53.000 --> 00:51:56.270 align:middle line:84%
That's not Riemann integrable,
but the sum of these integrals

00:51:56.270 --> 00:51:57.440 align:middle line:90%
is just 0.

00:51:57.440 --> 00:51:59.670 align:middle line:84%
So this is not true for
Riemann integrals again.

00:51:59.670 --> 00:52:03.125 align:middle line:84%
So we're doing something
much more powerful here.

00:52:03.125 --> 00:52:14.710 align:middle line:90%


00:52:14.710 --> 00:52:16.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:52:16.870 --> 00:52:26.960 align:middle line:84%
Let's do some more
properties of the integral.

00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:30.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, back to properties
of the integral.

00:52:30.670 --> 00:52:36.410 align:middle line:90%


00:52:36.410 --> 00:52:44.400 align:middle line:84%
So if I have a non-negative
measurable function,

00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:55.820 align:middle line:84%
then the integral of f equals
0 if and only if x equals 0

00:52:55.820 --> 00:53:11.120 align:middle line:84%
almost everywhere on
E. So now what's--

00:53:11.120 --> 00:53:14.300 align:middle line:84%
this is a two-way
street, so one direction.

00:53:14.300 --> 00:53:17.510 align:middle line:84%
If f is equal to 0
almost everywhere,

00:53:17.510 --> 00:53:20.762 align:middle line:84%
then it's less than or equal
to 0 almost everywhere.

00:53:20.762 --> 00:53:22.220 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
integral of f is

00:53:22.220 --> 00:53:24.830 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal
to the integral of 0,

00:53:24.830 --> 00:53:26.390 align:middle line:90%
and the integral of 0 is 0.

00:53:26.390 --> 00:53:36.530 align:middle line:84%
So this direction
follows from the fact

00:53:36.530 --> 00:53:40.400 align:middle line:84%
that f is less than or equal
to 0 almost everywhere,

00:53:40.400 --> 00:53:44.270 align:middle line:84%
which implies that the
integral of f over E

00:53:44.270 --> 00:53:48.020 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
the integral of 0, which

00:53:48.020 --> 00:53:49.040 align:middle line:90%
you can check is 0.

00:53:49.040 --> 00:53:52.090 align:middle line:90%


00:53:52.090 --> 00:53:54.760 align:middle line:84%
And this is a
non-negative quantity, so.

00:53:54.760 --> 00:53:57.360 align:middle line:90%


00:53:57.360 --> 00:54:00.060 align:middle line:84%
So now, how about
the other direction

00:54:00.060 --> 00:54:03.420 align:middle line:84%
that the integral of f being 0
for a non-negative measurable

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:09.090 align:middle line:84%
function implies f is
0 almost everywhere.

00:54:09.090 --> 00:54:18.030 align:middle line:84%
So let's let fn to be
the set of all x's and E

00:54:18.030 --> 00:54:24.525 align:middle line:84%
such that f of x is
bigger than 1 over n.

00:54:24.525 --> 00:54:29.910 align:middle line:90%


00:54:29.910 --> 00:54:37.540 align:middle line:84%
And let's let f be the set
of all x's such that f of x

00:54:37.540 --> 00:54:40.030 align:middle line:90%
is bigger than 0.

00:54:40.030 --> 00:54:46.460 align:middle line:84%
Now, if x is-- so this
is x and E, I should say.

00:54:46.460 --> 00:54:49.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, if I have an x where
f of x is bigger than 0,

00:54:49.340 --> 00:54:52.190 align:middle line:84%
then at least for
some large n, f

00:54:52.190 --> 00:55:05.330 align:middle line:84%
of x will be bigger than 1
over n So then the union from n

00:55:05.330 --> 00:55:09.275 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to infinity
of the fn's equals f.

00:55:09.275 --> 00:55:11.800 align:middle line:90%


00:55:11.800 --> 00:55:14.560 align:middle line:84%
I mean, each of these is
a subset of capital F,

00:55:14.560 --> 00:55:16.510 align:middle line:84%
so their union is
contained in capital F

00:55:16.510 --> 00:55:18.927 align:middle line:84%
and I've just-- the argument
I gave a minute ago shows you

00:55:18.927 --> 00:55:21.100 align:middle line:84%
that capital F is
contained in the union.

00:55:21.100 --> 00:55:25.390 align:middle line:90%
So this union equals f.

00:55:25.390 --> 00:55:32.190 align:middle line:84%
And just by how it's
defined, 1 is bigger

00:55:32.190 --> 00:55:36.540 align:middle line:84%
than or equal to a 1/2,
which is bigger than the 1/3,

00:55:36.540 --> 00:55:42.930 align:middle line:84%
f1 is contained in f2 is
contained in f3 and so on.

00:55:42.930 --> 00:55:47.160 align:middle line:84%
If f of x is bigger than 1/2,
it's certainly bigger than 1/3.

00:55:47.160 --> 00:55:51.560 align:middle line:90%


00:55:51.560 --> 00:55:59.960 align:middle line:84%
Right, so now we'll use again,
continuity of Lebesgue measure.

00:55:59.960 --> 00:56:08.030 align:middle line:84%
So then for all n, 0, which is
less than or equal to 1 over n

00:56:08.030 --> 00:56:10.160 align:middle line:90%
times the measure of fn--

00:56:10.160 --> 00:56:19.020 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to the
integral over E of 1

00:56:19.020 --> 00:56:22.500 align:middle line:84%
over n times the
indicator function of fn.

00:56:22.500 --> 00:56:29.710 align:middle line:90%
Now, on this set--

00:56:29.710 --> 00:56:32.420 align:middle line:90%
but what am I saying?

00:56:32.420 --> 00:56:33.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, let's write it this way.

00:56:33.670 --> 00:56:36.720 align:middle line:90%


00:56:36.720 --> 00:56:40.740 align:middle line:90%
This is equal to 1 over n.

00:56:40.740 --> 00:56:43.500 align:middle line:90%


00:56:43.500 --> 00:56:48.720 align:middle line:84%
And now, on fn, 1 over n is
less than or equal to f of x.

00:56:48.720 --> 00:56:51.210 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than or
equal to the integral

00:56:51.210 --> 00:56:56.400 align:middle line:84%
of fn F. And capital F sub
n is a subset of capital E,

00:56:56.400 --> 00:57:02.700 align:middle line:84%
so this is less than or equal to
the integral of E over E of f.

00:57:02.700 --> 00:57:06.690 align:middle line:84%
But by assumption,
this is 0, right?

00:57:06.690 --> 00:57:10.410 align:middle line:84%
And sandwiched in between
0 and 0 is 1 over n times

00:57:10.410 --> 00:57:12.390 align:middle line:90%
the measure of f sub n.

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:16.800 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, for all
n, measure of f sub n

00:57:16.800 --> 00:57:22.260 align:middle line:84%
equals 0, which tells me
that the measure of f-- which

00:57:22.260 --> 00:57:32.010 align:middle line:84%
is equal to that union, which
is equal to this increasing

00:57:32.010 --> 00:57:35.820 align:middle line:84%
union-- is by the continuity
of Lebesgue measure,

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:38.460 align:middle line:84%
equal to the limit
as n goes to infinity

00:57:38.460 --> 00:57:44.050 align:middle line:84%
of the measure of f
sub n, which equals 0.

00:57:44.050 --> 00:57:47.950 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the set of all
x's where f of x is positive

00:57:47.950 --> 00:57:51.325 align:middle line:90%
has Lebesgue measure 0.

00:57:51.325 --> 00:57:54.970 align:middle line:90%


00:57:54.970 --> 00:57:58.393 align:middle line:84%
And f equals 0
almost everywhere.

00:57:58.393 --> 00:58:04.190 align:middle line:90%


00:58:04.190 --> 00:58:06.890 align:middle line:84%
Now, using what we've done here
and the monotone convergence

00:58:06.890 --> 00:58:12.140 align:middle line:84%
theorem, we can slightly
relax the assumptions

00:58:12.140 --> 00:58:13.640 align:middle line:84%
in the monotone
convergence theorem.

00:58:13.640 --> 00:58:17.470 align:middle line:90%


00:58:17.470 --> 00:58:18.940 align:middle line:84%
So we have the
following theorem.

00:58:18.940 --> 00:58:32.500 align:middle line:84%
If fn is sequenced in
non-negative measurable

00:58:32.500 --> 00:58:44.220 align:middle line:84%
functions such that now
for almost every x in E,

00:58:44.220 --> 00:58:49.950 align:middle line:84%
we have f1 of x is less than or
equal to f2 of x is less than

00:58:49.950 --> 00:58:53.970 align:middle line:90%
or equal to f3 of x and so on.

00:58:53.970 --> 00:58:58.410 align:middle line:84%
And limit as n goes
to infinity of fn

00:58:58.410 --> 00:59:03.090 align:middle line:90%
of x equals a function f of x.

00:59:03.090 --> 00:59:06.510 align:middle line:84%
So remember, in the statement
of the monotone convergence

00:59:06.510 --> 00:59:08.970 align:middle line:84%
theorem, we assume these
two things for every x.

00:59:08.970 --> 00:59:12.857 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're just assuming
them for almost every x in E.

00:59:12.857 --> 00:59:14.190 align:middle line:90%
Then we get the same conclusion.

00:59:14.190 --> 00:59:20.310 align:middle line:84%
Then the integral of E of
f is equal to the limit

00:59:20.310 --> 00:59:23.790 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity
of the integral of E

00:59:23.790 --> 00:59:26.070 align:middle line:90%
over E of f sub n.

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:40.160 align:middle line:90%


00:59:40.160 --> 00:59:40.880 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:59:40.880 --> 00:59:46.235 align:middle line:84%
So we call these
two conditions star.

00:59:46.235 --> 00:59:51.550 align:middle line:90%


00:59:51.550 --> 00:59:57.730 align:middle line:84%
Let capital F be the
set while x is in E such

00:59:57.730 --> 01:00:03.865 align:middle line:84%
that fn's are increasing
to F, so star holds.

01:00:03.865 --> 01:00:07.950 align:middle line:90%


01:00:07.950 --> 01:00:11.920 align:middle line:84%
Then the measure
of the complement

01:00:11.920 --> 01:00:14.900 align:middle line:90%
is, by assumption, equal to 0.

01:00:14.900 --> 01:00:16.760 align:middle line:84%
And I should say here,
the complement in E.

01:00:16.760 --> 01:00:18.485 align:middle line:90%
So I should say E take away f.

01:00:18.485 --> 01:00:21.630 align:middle line:90%


01:00:21.630 --> 01:00:28.590 align:middle line:84%
So let me-- and that
should have been in the--

01:00:28.590 --> 01:00:31.120 align:middle line:90%


01:00:31.120 --> 01:00:32.620 align:middle line:84%
so if I write
complement, you should

01:00:32.620 --> 01:00:35.840 align:middle line:84%
interpret that as the complement
within E. So E take away f.

01:00:35.840 --> 01:00:40.340 align:middle line:90%


01:00:40.340 --> 01:00:48.050 align:middle line:90%
Then f minus chi sub f--

01:00:48.050 --> 01:00:50.540 align:middle line:84%
so this is the indicator
function over capital F.

01:00:50.540 --> 01:00:55.940 align:middle line:90%
This equals 0 almost everywhere.

01:00:55.940 --> 01:01:07.550 align:middle line:84%
And fn minus chi sub f fn equals
0 almost everywhere for all n.

01:01:07.550 --> 01:01:13.780 align:middle line:84%
These equal 0 when
x is an f which

01:01:13.780 --> 01:01:16.090 align:middle line:84%
the complement is
as measure 0, which

01:01:16.090 --> 01:01:17.440 align:middle line:90%
I didn't finish writing down.

01:01:17.440 --> 01:01:27.400 align:middle line:90%


01:01:27.400 --> 01:01:33.550 align:middle line:84%
Now, by monotone convergence
theorem applied now

01:01:33.550 --> 01:01:48.120 align:middle line:84%
to these parts, if you like,
and the previous theorem, we

01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:58.930 align:middle line:84%
have that the
integral of f of E,

01:01:58.930 --> 01:02:05.620 align:middle line:90%
this is equal to the chi sub f.

01:02:05.620 --> 01:02:10.960 align:middle line:90%


01:02:10.960 --> 01:02:16.550 align:middle line:90%
And so this is equal to--

01:02:16.550 --> 01:02:21.050 align:middle line:90%
so since f minus f--

01:02:21.050 --> 01:02:21.550 align:middle line:90%
wait.

01:02:21.550 --> 01:02:26.410 align:middle line:90%


01:02:26.410 --> 01:02:27.700 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so OK.

01:02:27.700 --> 01:02:32.030 align:middle line:84%
So since f equals f times
chi sub f almost everywhere,

01:02:32.030 --> 01:02:34.460 align:middle line:90%
the integrals equal.

01:02:34.460 --> 01:02:36.580 align:middle line:84%
And so this is equal
to the integral

01:02:36.580 --> 01:02:39.040 align:middle line:90%
over capital F of little f.

01:02:39.040 --> 01:02:42.250 align:middle line:84%
And by the monotone convergence
theorem applied here,

01:02:42.250 --> 01:02:45.370 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to
the limit as n goes

01:02:45.370 --> 01:02:53.000 align:middle line:84%
to infinity of the fn's,
because they are pointwise

01:02:53.000 --> 01:02:55.370 align:middle line:84%
increasing on capital
F to little f,

01:02:55.370 --> 01:02:58.005 align:middle line:90%
and this is equal to--

01:02:58.005 --> 01:03:00.050 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I really didn't
need the previous theorem.

01:03:00.050 --> 01:03:01.550 align:middle line:84%
I could have used
what I had earlier

01:03:01.550 --> 01:03:07.580 align:middle line:84%
that if I have two functions
which equal each other almost

01:03:07.580 --> 01:03:09.110 align:middle line:90%
everywhere.

01:03:09.110 --> 01:03:11.150 align:middle line:84%
So this previous
theorem should not

01:03:11.150 --> 01:03:14.060 align:middle line:84%
be referring to what I just
proved a minute ago, but really

01:03:14.060 --> 01:03:15.950 align:middle line:84%
to the theorem at the
beginning of lecture

01:03:15.950 --> 01:03:18.325 align:middle line:84%
that if I have two functions
that equal each other almost

01:03:18.325 --> 01:03:21.290 align:middle line:84%
everywhere, then their
integrals equal each other.

01:03:21.290 --> 01:03:24.440 align:middle line:90%


01:03:24.440 --> 01:03:25.940 align:middle line:84%
Although, maybe I
didn't state that.

01:03:25.940 --> 01:03:27.607 align:middle line:84%
I just stated the
less than or equal to.

01:03:27.607 --> 01:03:29.630 align:middle line:84%
But if they're equal
almost everywhere,

01:03:29.630 --> 01:03:32.300 align:middle line:84%
they're less than or equal to
each other almost everywhere.

01:03:32.300 --> 01:03:33.500 align:middle line:90%
Anyways, back to this.

01:03:33.500 --> 01:03:40.850 align:middle line:90%
This is equal to the limit fn.

01:03:40.850 --> 01:03:44.860 align:middle line:84%
So the whole point is
that sets of measure 0

01:03:44.860 --> 01:03:49.570 align:middle line:84%
don't affect statements
that involve integrals.

01:03:49.570 --> 01:03:57.140 align:middle line:84%
That should be the take home,
that if your conclusions are

01:03:57.140 --> 01:04:00.320 align:middle line:84%
in terms of
integrals, conditions

01:04:00.320 --> 01:04:04.940 align:middle line:84%
holding almost everywhere
suffice, typically.

01:04:04.940 --> 01:04:07.130 align:middle line:84%
So for example, the
simplest one we had earlier

01:04:07.130 --> 01:04:09.667 align:middle line:84%
was that if f is less
than or equal to g,

01:04:09.667 --> 01:04:11.750 align:middle line:84%
then the integral of f is
less than or equal to g.

01:04:11.750 --> 01:04:14.450 align:middle line:84%
We can relax that
to the integral

01:04:14.450 --> 01:04:16.790 align:middle line:84%
that if f is less than or
equal to g almost everywhere,

01:04:16.790 --> 01:04:19.440 align:middle line:84%
then the conclusion, which is
stated in terms of integrals,

01:04:19.440 --> 01:04:19.940 align:middle line:90%
still holds.

01:04:19.940 --> 01:04:22.482 align:middle line:84%
The integral of f is less than
or equal to the integral of g.

01:04:22.482 --> 01:04:29.660 align:middle line:90%


01:04:29.660 --> 01:04:49.730 align:middle line:84%
So now, we'll do the second
big convergence of integrals--

01:04:49.730 --> 01:04:56.390 align:middle line:84%
or this one's actually an
inequality between integrals,

01:04:56.390 --> 01:04:58.590 align:middle line:90%
but it's still extremely useful.

01:04:58.590 --> 01:05:01.790 align:middle line:84%
In fact, it's equivalent to the
monotone convergence theorem,

01:05:01.790 --> 01:05:04.640 align:middle line:84%
so it is neither
stronger nor weaker.

01:05:04.640 --> 01:05:17.740 align:middle line:90%


01:05:17.740 --> 01:05:28.930 align:middle line:84%
So we have Fatou's lemma,
stated as a theorem,

01:05:28.930 --> 01:05:41.790 align:middle line:84%
of course which
states that if fn

01:05:41.790 --> 01:05:50.220 align:middle line:84%
is a sequence in L plus of
E, then the integral of E

01:05:50.220 --> 01:05:59.020 align:middle line:84%
of the liminf is the integral
that's infinity of fn of x.

01:05:59.020 --> 01:06:01.870 align:middle line:90%
This is a function.

01:06:01.870 --> 01:06:06.940 align:middle line:84%
For each x, I take the liminf as
n goes to infinity of fn of x.

01:06:06.940 --> 01:06:12.190 align:middle line:84%
This is less than or
equal to the liminf

01:06:12.190 --> 01:06:17.650 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity
of the integrals of fn.

01:06:17.650 --> 01:06:22.980 align:middle line:90%
So let me state it this way.

01:06:22.980 --> 01:06:27.900 align:middle line:84%
So the liminf of f
sub n, let me just

01:06:27.900 --> 01:06:29.780 align:middle line:90%
recall, what is the liminf?

01:06:29.780 --> 01:06:36.250 align:middle line:90%


01:06:36.250 --> 01:06:48.270 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to the sup
over n equals 1 inf over k

01:06:48.270 --> 01:06:54.460 align:middle line:84%
bigger than or
equal to n fk of x.

01:06:54.460 --> 01:06:59.470 align:middle line:84%
So that is the definition of
the limsup, if you like, if--

01:06:59.470 --> 01:07:02.560 align:middle line:84%
in fact, let me not
just be specific to fn

01:07:02.560 --> 01:07:09.310 align:middle line:84%
of x, just of a sequence of real
numbers, the liminf of a sub n

01:07:09.310 --> 01:07:12.610 align:middle line:84%
is equal to this thing
on the right hand side.

01:07:12.610 --> 01:07:23.750 align:middle line:90%


01:07:23.750 --> 01:07:26.030 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:07:26.030 --> 01:07:31.760 align:middle line:84%
So this follows pretty easily
from the monotone convergence

01:07:31.760 --> 01:07:32.857 align:middle line:90%
theorem.

01:07:32.857 --> 01:07:34.940 align:middle line:84%
I said a minute ago that
it's, in fact, equivalent

01:07:34.940 --> 01:07:36.440 align:middle line:84%
to the monotone
convergence theorem.

01:07:36.440 --> 01:07:39.500 align:middle line:84%
You can prove if
you-- so we're going

01:07:39.500 --> 01:07:41.840 align:middle line:84%
to use the monotone convergence
theorem to prove it.

01:07:41.840 --> 01:07:44.120 align:middle line:84%
You can also assume
Fatou's lemma holds

01:07:44.120 --> 01:07:50.180 align:middle line:84%
and then prove the monotone
convergence theorem from it.

01:07:50.180 --> 01:07:54.420 align:middle line:84%
You can also prove
it independently

01:07:54.420 --> 01:07:56.730 align:middle line:84%
from the monotone
convergence theorem.

01:07:56.730 --> 01:07:59.370 align:middle line:84%
I mean, using essentially
what's a similar argument to how

01:07:59.370 --> 01:08:03.370 align:middle line:84%
you prove the monotone
convergence theorem.

01:08:03.370 --> 01:08:20.983 align:middle line:84%
OK, so first off,
so we have liminf

01:08:20.983 --> 01:08:26.359 align:middle line:84%
of fn of x, which is, again,
by what I've written up here,

01:08:26.359 --> 01:08:32.319 align:middle line:84%
sup n bigger than or equal
to 1 inf k bigger than

01:08:32.319 --> 01:08:37.300 align:middle line:90%
or equal to n fk of x.

01:08:37.300 --> 01:08:41.620 align:middle line:90%
This is now for a fixed n--

01:08:41.620 --> 01:08:48.720 align:middle line:84%
or what happens to what's in
the bracket as n is increasing?

01:08:48.720 --> 01:08:52.350 align:middle line:84%
Well, this inf is being
taken over a smaller set.

01:08:52.350 --> 01:08:54.810 align:middle line:84%
And the inf of a smaller
set is bigger than

01:08:54.810 --> 01:08:57.689 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the inf
of the larger set.

01:08:57.689 --> 01:09:04.649 align:middle line:84%
So this inf here, this thing in
brackets, is increasing in n.

01:09:04.649 --> 01:09:09.279 align:middle line:84%
So this sup is,
in fact, the limit

01:09:09.279 --> 01:09:20.310 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity
of this increasing

01:09:20.310 --> 01:09:23.279 align:middle line:84%
sequence of real numbers
defined as the inf over k bigger

01:09:23.279 --> 01:09:27.470 align:middle line:90%
than or equal to n of fk of x.

01:09:27.470 --> 01:09:30.620 align:middle line:84%
And so what I just told you
is not specific to fk of x.

01:09:30.620 --> 01:09:34.939 align:middle line:84%
It's specific to
ak, for sequence ak.

01:09:34.939 --> 01:09:39.521 align:middle line:90%


01:09:39.521 --> 01:09:48.408 align:middle line:84%
OK, and basically, I'm
going to write down

01:09:48.408 --> 01:09:49.450 align:middle line:90%
what I said a minute ago.

01:09:49.450 --> 01:09:54.340 align:middle line:84%
Since fk bigger than
or equal to 1 fk of x

01:09:54.340 --> 01:09:58.884 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to inf
a bigger than or equal to 2.

01:09:58.884 --> 01:10:02.355 align:middle line:84%
We have fk of x is less
than or equal to-- now

01:10:02.355 --> 01:10:06.430 align:middle line:90%
it changes to 3 and so on.

01:10:06.430 --> 01:10:12.960 align:middle line:84%
This implies by the
monotone convergence theorem

01:10:12.960 --> 01:10:23.860 align:middle line:84%
that the integral of the liminf
of fn is equal to the limit

01:10:23.860 --> 01:10:29.070 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity of the
integral over E of the inf

01:10:29.070 --> 01:10:32.710 align:middle line:90%
k bigger than or equal to n fk.

01:10:32.710 --> 01:10:35.870 align:middle line:84%
So I have this function here,
which is defined in this way.

01:10:35.870 --> 01:10:38.080 align:middle line:84%
So for each n, I
get a function here.

01:10:38.080 --> 01:10:49.750 align:middle line:90%


01:10:49.750 --> 01:10:50.470 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:10:50.470 --> 01:10:58.390 align:middle line:84%
Now, for all j bigger
than or equal to m,

01:10:58.390 --> 01:11:03.310 align:middle line:84%
this function given
by the inf over k

01:11:03.310 --> 01:11:06.070 align:middle line:90%
bigger than or equal to--

01:11:06.070 --> 01:11:09.710 align:middle line:84%
let me add one more
quantifier in here.

01:11:09.710 --> 01:11:14.380 align:middle line:84%
So for all j bigger than or
equal to n, for all x in E,

01:11:14.380 --> 01:11:19.450 align:middle line:84%
I have that the inf over
k bigger than or equal to

01:11:19.450 --> 01:11:22.270 align:middle line:90%
n of fk of x--

01:11:22.270 --> 01:11:26.435 align:middle line:84%
this is certainly less
than or equal to fj of x.

01:11:26.435 --> 01:11:29.340 align:middle line:90%


01:11:29.340 --> 01:11:32.400 align:middle line:84%
This is the inf
overall fk of x for k

01:11:32.400 --> 01:11:33.600 align:middle line:90%
bigger than or equal to n.

01:11:33.600 --> 01:11:36.900 align:middle line:84%
And for any fixed j bigger than
or equal to n, that's certainly

01:11:36.900 --> 01:11:38.490 align:middle line:90%
less than or equal to fj of x.

01:11:38.490 --> 01:11:40.290 align:middle line:84%
Because this is a
lower bound for all

01:11:40.290 --> 01:11:44.070 align:middle line:84%
of these guys for all j
bigger than or equal to n.

01:11:44.070 --> 01:11:47.040 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, since
this function here

01:11:47.040 --> 01:11:52.950 align:middle line:84%
sits below this function, I have
for all j bigger than or equal

01:11:52.950 --> 01:11:59.880 align:middle line:84%
to n, the integral
of E of inf of fk

01:11:59.880 --> 01:12:03.680 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the integral of fj.

01:12:03.680 --> 01:12:06.530 align:middle line:90%


01:12:06.530 --> 01:12:10.910 align:middle line:84%
So I have this number here
sits below this number

01:12:10.910 --> 01:12:12.830 align:middle line:90%
here for all j.

01:12:12.830 --> 01:12:16.310 align:middle line:84%
This is a fixed
number depending on n,

01:12:16.310 --> 01:12:19.370 align:middle line:84%
this is a fixed
number depending on j.

01:12:19.370 --> 01:12:21.740 align:middle line:84%
And this holds for all j
bigger than or equal to n.

01:12:21.740 --> 01:12:24.470 align:middle line:84%
So this thing has
to be a lower bound

01:12:24.470 --> 01:12:27.080 align:middle line:84%
for the set of all
numbers of this form for j

01:12:27.080 --> 01:12:28.640 align:middle line:90%
bigger than or equal to n.

01:12:28.640 --> 01:12:33.080 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the integral
of E of inf k bigger

01:12:33.080 --> 01:12:37.190 align:middle line:84%
than or equal to n of fk is
less than or equal to the inf

01:12:37.190 --> 01:12:45.360 align:middle line:84%
overall j bigger than or equal
to n of the integral of f sub

01:12:45.360 --> 01:12:50.610 align:middle line:84%
j over E. Now, we're going
to take this and stick it

01:12:50.610 --> 01:12:53.445 align:middle line:90%
into this inequality here.

01:12:53.445 --> 01:13:02.388 align:middle line:90%


01:13:02.388 --> 01:13:04.850 align:middle line:84%
So that's what we
had before, which

01:13:04.850 --> 01:13:13.820 align:middle line:84%
was that the liminf of fn over
E, which is equal to limit as n

01:13:13.820 --> 01:13:23.060 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of the inf k
bigger than or equal to n fk.

01:13:23.060 --> 01:13:25.190 align:middle line:84%
This is, by what
we've just shown,

01:13:25.190 --> 01:13:26.690 align:middle line:84%
is less than or
equal to the limit

01:13:26.690 --> 01:13:31.550 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity
of the inf over j bigger

01:13:31.550 --> 01:13:35.850 align:middle line:90%
than or equal to n of fj.

01:13:35.850 --> 01:13:38.680 align:middle line:90%


01:13:38.680 --> 01:13:46.120 align:middle line:84%
But this is just, by
definition, equal to the liminf

01:13:46.120 --> 01:13:50.850 align:middle line:84%
of the integrals of the
fn's, which is Fatou's lemma.

01:13:50.850 --> 01:14:05.380 align:middle line:90%


01:14:05.380 --> 01:14:13.900 align:middle line:84%
OK, so one more theorem
about the Lebesgue integral,

01:14:13.900 --> 01:14:17.010 align:middle line:90%
which is a very useful one.

01:14:17.010 --> 01:14:19.200 align:middle line:84%
Throughout all this,
we have had functions

01:14:19.200 --> 01:14:20.980 align:middle line:90%
that are extended real value.

01:14:20.980 --> 01:14:24.300 align:middle line:84%
So we're dealing with
non-negative functions, which

01:14:24.300 --> 01:14:27.750 align:middle line:90%
can equal infinity at points.

01:14:27.750 --> 01:14:29.370 align:middle line:84%
And maybe that
makes you nervous,

01:14:29.370 --> 01:14:32.580 align:middle line:84%
but I'm going to
tell you that as long

01:14:32.580 --> 01:14:34.170 align:middle line:84%
as the integral is
finite, you don't

01:14:34.170 --> 01:14:36.960 align:middle line:90%
have to be nervous too often.

01:14:36.960 --> 01:14:43.260 align:middle line:84%
So if f is a
non-negative measurable

01:14:43.260 --> 01:14:54.800 align:middle line:84%
function over a measurable set
E and the integral is finite,

01:14:54.800 --> 01:15:00.980 align:middle line:84%
then the set of
x is where f of x

01:15:00.980 --> 01:15:09.065 align:middle line:84%
is infinite as the
set of measure 0.

01:15:09.065 --> 01:15:11.720 align:middle line:90%


01:15:11.720 --> 01:15:15.170 align:middle line:84%
So the measure of the set
where it's infinite is 0.

01:15:15.170 --> 01:15:16.130 align:middle line:90%
So what's the proof?

01:15:16.130 --> 01:15:23.540 align:middle line:90%


01:15:23.540 --> 01:15:31.190 align:middle line:84%
So it's kind of how
we did, in spirit,

01:15:31.190 --> 01:15:36.300 align:middle line:84%
the proof that if
the integral is 0,

01:15:36.300 --> 01:15:38.620 align:middle line:84%
then the function is
0 almost everywhere.

01:15:38.620 --> 01:15:42.960 align:middle line:84%
So let f be the
set of all x in E

01:15:42.960 --> 01:15:51.600 align:middle line:84%
such that f of x equals
infinity and fn be

01:15:51.600 --> 01:15:57.270 align:middle line:84%
the set of all x's in E such
that f of x is bigger than n.

01:15:57.270 --> 01:16:08.090 align:middle line:90%


01:16:08.090 --> 01:16:11.087 align:middle line:84%
Oh well, that's what
I had in my mind,

01:16:11.087 --> 01:16:12.920 align:middle line:84%
but what I wrote in my
notes is a little bit

01:16:12.920 --> 01:16:15.770 align:middle line:84%
different than the proof
I had in my mind just now.

01:16:15.770 --> 01:16:18.830 align:middle line:84%
So let's go with what's
in my notes that's

01:16:18.830 --> 01:16:21.170 align:middle line:90%
a little more cautious.

01:16:21.170 --> 01:16:21.670 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:16:21.670 --> 01:16:24.480 align:middle line:90%


01:16:24.480 --> 01:16:32.130 align:middle line:84%
Then for all n,
a natural number,

01:16:32.130 --> 01:16:38.430 align:middle line:84%
n times chi f is less than or
equal to f times chi f, where

01:16:38.430 --> 01:16:40.830 align:middle line:84%
this is the indicator
function of capital

01:16:40.830 --> 01:16:43.920 align:middle line:84%
F. Because f on capital
F is just infinite,

01:16:43.920 --> 01:16:46.710 align:middle line:90%
so this always holds, right?

01:16:46.710 --> 01:16:53.470 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, n times
the measure of f--

01:16:53.470 --> 01:16:57.960 align:middle line:84%
so of all n, n
times a measure of f

01:16:57.960 --> 01:17:05.010 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
integral over E times f chi f,

01:17:05.010 --> 01:17:06.840 align:middle line:84%
which is less than or
equal to the integral

01:17:06.840 --> 01:17:10.620 align:middle line:90%
of E of f, which is finite.

01:17:10.620 --> 01:17:11.655 align:middle line:90%
That's a fixed number.

01:17:11.655 --> 01:17:15.460 align:middle line:90%


01:17:15.460 --> 01:17:20.050 align:middle line:84%
Then for all n, the
measure of f is less than

01:17:20.050 --> 01:17:23.740 align:middle line:84%
or equal to 1 over
n times the integral

01:17:23.740 --> 01:17:28.510 align:middle line:84%
of f over E. Again, this is a
fixed finite number which goes

01:17:28.510 --> 01:17:30.500 align:middle line:90%
to 0 as n goes to infinity.

01:17:30.500 --> 01:17:32.005 align:middle line:84%
This is just a fixed
number as well.

01:17:32.005 --> 01:17:42.340 align:middle line:90%


01:17:42.340 --> 01:17:46.480 align:middle line:90%
Thus, measure of f equals 0.

01:17:46.480 --> 01:17:51.230 align:middle line:90%


01:17:51.230 --> 01:17:54.050 align:middle line:84%
OK, so that seems like
a good place to stop.

01:17:54.050 --> 01:17:58.220 align:middle line:84%
Next time-- so we've defined
the Lebesgue integral

01:17:58.220 --> 01:18:01.580 align:middle line:84%
of a non-negative
measurable function.

01:18:01.580 --> 01:18:06.410 align:middle line:84%
We will then define the class
of Lebesgue integrable functions

01:18:06.410 --> 01:18:08.930 align:middle line:84%
and extend the
definition of integral

01:18:08.930 --> 01:18:12.575 align:middle line:84%
to those functions in a
fairly straightforward way.

01:18:12.575 --> 01:18:15.910 align:middle line:90%


01:18:15.910 --> 01:18:19.895 align:middle line:84%
Prove some simple properties
of the Lebesgue integral.

01:18:19.895 --> 01:18:22.650 align:middle line:90%


01:18:22.650 --> 01:18:25.390 align:middle line:84%
And also, the last big
convergence theorem,

01:18:25.390 --> 01:18:27.690 align:middle line:84%
which is the dominated
convergence theorem.

01:18:27.690 --> 01:18:33.000 align:middle line:84%
And we may or may not finish
by the end of next lecture

01:18:33.000 --> 01:18:38.220 align:middle line:84%
the proof that Lp spaces,
which are based on the Lebesgue

01:18:38.220 --> 01:18:41.280 align:middle line:84%
integral-- so we built the
Lebesgue integral to have

01:18:41.280 --> 01:18:52.860 align:middle line:84%
a space of functions
for which kind of--

01:18:52.860 --> 01:18:53.730 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let me stop.

01:18:53.730 --> 01:18:57.020 align:middle line:90%
That alarm kind of threw me off.

01:18:57.020 --> 01:19:01.670 align:middle line:84%
So it's not too
difficult to show proof.

01:19:01.670 --> 01:19:04.430 align:middle line:84%
Or you can just accept for now--
and we'll actually see why this

01:19:04.430 --> 01:19:05.450 align:middle line:90%
is the case soon--

01:19:05.450 --> 01:19:09.800 align:middle line:84%
that the space of continuous
functions with norm

01:19:09.800 --> 01:19:11.160 align:middle line:90%
being the integral.

01:19:11.160 --> 01:19:14.300 align:middle line:84%
So the norm of f
being, let's say,

01:19:14.300 --> 01:19:18.740 align:middle line:84%
the integral of the absolute
value of f is a norm space,

01:19:18.740 --> 01:19:22.070 align:middle line:90%
but it's not a Banach space.

01:19:22.070 --> 01:19:25.790 align:middle line:84%
Or you could change the
integral of the absolute value

01:19:25.790 --> 01:19:30.350 align:middle line:84%
of f to what's called a big
Lp-norm, the integral of f

01:19:30.350 --> 01:19:32.870 align:middle line:84%
raised to the p all
raised to the 1 over p.

01:19:32.870 --> 01:19:34.970 align:middle line:84%
So the analog of the
little Lp norms, which

01:19:34.970 --> 01:19:38.900 align:middle line:90%
we encountered a few weeks ago.

01:19:38.900 --> 01:19:41.000 align:middle line:84%
None of those are
Banach spaces when

01:19:41.000 --> 01:19:43.010 align:middle line:84%
restricted to continuous
functions, or even

01:19:43.010 --> 01:19:45.810 align:middle line:90%
Riemann integrable functions.

01:19:45.810 --> 01:19:50.870 align:middle line:84%
So our goal-- at least
it was a while back

01:19:50.870 --> 01:19:54.530 align:middle line:84%
when we started this section,
this big section on Lebesgue

01:19:54.530 --> 01:20:01.460 align:middle line:84%
integration-- was to build,
or at least come upon a space

01:20:01.460 --> 01:20:06.330 align:middle line:84%
where the resulting integrable
functions form a Banach space.

01:20:06.330 --> 01:20:10.160 align:middle line:84%
And so we may or may not,
by the end of next lecture,

01:20:10.160 --> 01:20:11.430 align:middle line:90%
introduce those.

01:20:11.430 --> 01:20:16.650 align:middle line:90%
But that's where we're headed.

01:20:16.650 --> 01:20:18.200 align:middle line:90%
That's where we're almost at.

01:20:18.200 --> 01:20:23.450 align:middle line:84%
And these spaces
arose because one

01:20:23.450 --> 01:20:27.800 align:middle line:84%
wants to apply functional
analysis facts, tools

01:20:27.800 --> 01:20:31.700 align:middle line:84%
to concrete questions, such
as questions about convergence

01:20:31.700 --> 01:20:38.780 align:middle line:84%
of Fourier series, which arose
immediately after Fourier said

01:20:38.780 --> 01:20:44.200 align:middle line:84%
that any periodic function
can be expanded as a Fourier

01:20:44.200 --> 01:20:44.700 align:middle line:90%
series.

01:20:44.700 --> 01:20:46.640 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of people went
to a lot of trouble

01:20:46.640 --> 01:20:54.630 align:middle line:84%
to fill in precisely what it
means expanded as, expanded

01:20:54.630 --> 01:20:56.340 align:middle line:90%
as pointwise.

01:20:56.340 --> 01:21:00.080 align:middle line:84%
Does this Fourier
series converge

01:21:00.080 --> 01:21:04.610 align:middle line:84%
to the function that was kind
of hard to do on average?

01:21:04.610 --> 01:21:06.890 align:middle line:84%
Do you mean average as
in measured with respect

01:21:06.890 --> 01:21:11.300 align:middle line:84%
to some norm that's integrated
that involves integration?

01:21:11.300 --> 01:21:15.320 align:middle line:84%
So which is why
we're coming here.

01:21:15.320 --> 01:21:18.380 align:middle line:90%
But we'll see that next time.

01:21:18.380 --> 01:21:23.120 align:middle line:84%
Or we'll see the applications
of this integration theory,

01:21:23.120 --> 01:21:25.280 align:middle line:84%
along with the
functional analysis later

01:21:25.280 --> 01:21:29.240 align:middle line:84%
in the course when we circle
around to Fourier series.

01:21:29.240 --> 01:21:31.630 align:middle line:90%
All right, so we'll stop there.

01:21:31.630 --> 01:21:38.000 align:middle line:90%